<BaseLayout
  title="WIP presenter guide"
  description="A friendly guide to sharing unfinished work and getting useful feedback at EDM Megalab meetings."
>
  <article class="guide-copy shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Presenter guide · Works in progress</p>
    <h1>Sharing Work in Progress with EDM Megalab</h1>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      Bring the work <strong>as it is</strong>, not as you think a formal talk ought to
      look. Preliminary ideas, puzzling results, half-built models, design forks, one
      troublesome figure, and analyses that did not work are all welcome. You do not need
      a complete story or beautiful slides.
    </p>
    <p>
      What does help is a clear purpose. A useful WIP is <strong
        >prepared, not polished</strong
      >{""}: give EDM Megalab enough context to think with you, and identify one to three
      questions or goals that will make the conversation useful.
    </p>

    <h2>What a WIP is for</h2>
    <p>
      A WIP is a working session, not a performance or an exam. Its purpose is to make the
      science better while there is still time to change it. Presenting early is often
      especially valuable: advice about a design, analysis, or model can have much more
      impact before the next step is locked in.
    </p>
    <p>Good reasons to present include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        choosing between two task designs, models, measures, or analysis strategies;
      </li>
      <li>stress-testing an assumption or interpretation;</li>
      <li>troubleshooting an unexpected result or failed analysis;</li>
      <li>
        deciding which diagnostic, simulation, or follow-up study would be most
        informative;
      </li>
      <li>checking whether a scientific question and design line up;</li>
      <li>finding relevant expertise, tools, datasets, or collaborators;</li>
      <li>getting reactions to the emerging story of a paper or grant; or</li>
      <li>rehearsing a talk, if presentation feedback is the explicit goal.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The work can be at any stage. It only needs to be developed enough that EDM Megalab
      has something concrete to discuss.
    </p>

    <h2>Start with the help you want</h2>
    <p>Before making slides, finish this sentence:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>By the end of this session, I would like to have a clearer answer about...</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Then turn that goal into one to three focal questions. Questions that name a
      decision, uncertainty, or tradeoff tend to produce more useful feedback than an
      open-ended request for general thoughts.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Instead of only asking...</th>
          <th>Give EDM Megalab a focal question such as...</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>“What do you think?”</td>
          <td>
            “Which of these two task designs better separates learning from choice
            variability, and what would each fail to identify?”
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>“Does the model look okay?”</td>
          <td>
            “Are the state and trait components identifiable with this sampling schedule?
            What recovery check would be most informative?”
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>“Any ideas about this result?”</td>
          <td>
            “What plausible artifact or alternative explanation should we rule out before
            interpreting this effect?”
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>“Is the project ready?”</td>
          <td>
            “What is the highest-risk unresolved decision before we preregister and begin
            data collection?”
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>“How are my slides?”</td>
          <td>
            “After this short explanation, can you state the main claim in one sentence?
            Which figure or transition obscured it?”
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      It is also helpful to say what is <strong>open for discussion</strong>, what is
      already
      <strong>fixed</strong>, and what is <strong>outside the scope</strong> of this session.
      That keeps the discussion from spending twenty minutes redesigning a part of the project
      that cannot change.
    </p>

    <h2>Send a short agenda note</h2>
    <p>
      When arranging the session, send the coordinator a short note that can be used to
      build and circulate the meeting agenda. It can be brief and informal:
    </p>
    <pre><code>Working title:
Project stage:
One-sentence scientific question:
What I want from the session (1-3 focal questions or goals):
What I plan to show:
Discussion preference (interruptions welcome, clarifications only, or hold
  questions until a stopping point):
Useful expertise to have in the room:
Any confidentiality or sharing constraints:</code></pre>
    <p>
      This lets the coordinator allocate time, combine WIPs thoughtfully, invite people
      with relevant expertise, and keep the discussion centered on the presenter's goals.
      If the session contains more than one WIP, the agenda should state the time
      available to each presenter and protect a discussion block for each project.
    </p>

    <h2>Prepare only what the discussion needs</h2>
    <p>
      There is no required slide count or deck structure. You can use rough slides, a
      whiteboard, a notebook, a design diagram, a live analysis, a handout, or a single
      figure. Aesthetics are optional; legibility is not. Label axes, define unfamiliar
      terms, and give a cross-lab audience enough orientation to take part.
    </p>
    <p>A simple WIP often needs only:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The question and the ask.</strong> What are you studying, and where do you
        want help from EDM Megalab?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Just enough context.</strong> What does EDM Megalab need to know to reason
        about the decision? Skip the exhaustive literature review.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The current state of the work.</strong> Show the design, data, code, result,
        or competing options honestly. Label preliminary results as preliminary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The decision space.</strong> What alternatives have you considered? What are
        their tradeoffs or constraints?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The focal questions again.</strong> Put them back on the screen when discussion
        begins.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      For a full 90-minute WIP session, a useful default is roughly 20–30 minutes of
      framing and 40–50 minutes of discussion, with a little room for introductions,
      transitions, and a closing summary. If two projects share a meeting, the coordinator
      and presenters should scale this together. The underlying principle is more
      important than the exact numbers: <strong
        >stop presenting while there is still ample time to work on the questions.</strong
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      If the project is complicated, consider sharing one figure, diagram, abstract, or
      short note in advance. Pre-reading should be helpful rather than a hidden admission
      requirement; the session should still make sense to someone who did not read it.
    </p>

    <h2>Open by making the contract clear</h2>
    <p>At the beginning, tell the room how to help. For example:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        This is an early pilot, and the main thing I need today is help deciding whether
        the next version should use a within- or between-person manipulation. I will give
        about ten minutes of context, show the tradeoffs I see, and then pause. Clarifying
        questions are welcome as I go; could we hold broader design alternatives until
        that pause?
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      You are allowed to redirect the conversation. “That is an important issue, but it is
      fixed for this dataset; could we return to the model-comparison question?” is good
      facilitation, not defensiveness.
    </p>
    <p>
      You also do not need an immediate answer to every question or a defense of every
      preliminary choice. “I do not know,” “that is exactly the uncertainty I wanted help
      with,” and “let me think about that after the meeting” are all perfectly good WIP
      responses.
    </p>

    <h2>Make feedback a shared responsibility</h2>
    <p>
      A low-polish WIP format works only when the room is constructive. Audience members
      should:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>listen for the presenter's stated goals and address those questions first;</li>
      <li>ask clarifying questions before prescribing a solution;</li>
      <li>distinguish a serious concern from an optional idea or personal preference;</li>
      <li>critique the design, evidence, or claim rather than the presenter;</li>
      <li>make room for people from different career stages and areas of expertise;</li>
      <li>keep comments concise enough for a real conversation;</li>
      <li>avoid turning someone else's WIP into a presentation of their own work; and</li>
      <li>respect any confidentiality or sharing boundary stated by the presenter.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Critical feedback is welcome and often the point. “Constructive” does not mean
      avoiding disagreement; it means making disagreement specific, respectful, and
      useful. When possible, pair a concern with a diagnostic, alternative, or next step.
    </p>
    <p>
      The facilitator or coordinator should briefly restate the presenter's goals, watch
      the agenda and the clock, bring the discussion back when it drifts, invite voices
      that have not yet entered, and reserve a few minutes to summarize. A volunteer
      note-taker can be extremely helpful: the presenter is then free to listen and think
      instead of trying to capture every comment.
    </p>

    <h2>End with a short harvest</h2>
    <p>Use the last few minutes to ask:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What are the strongest points of agreement?</li>
      <li>Where does uncertainty or disagreement remain?</li>
      <li>What are one or two promising next steps?</li>
      <li>Is there someone the presenter should follow up with?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Afterward, the presenter decides what to use. Feedback is input, not a vote or a
      list of required revisions. Review the notes while the conversation is fresh, record
      the suggestions you want to pursue, and keep track of why you set others aside.
      Follow up individually when a contribution opens a useful technical question or
      collaboration.
    </p>

    <h2>A final reassurance</h2>
    <p>
      Please do not postpone a WIP because the slides are not attractive, the results are
      messy, or the story has not come together. Those may be signs that this is exactly
      the right time to present. The courtesy you owe others is not visual polish; it is a
      focused agenda, honest context, and enough time for discussion.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources and examples from other research communities</h2>
    <p>This guide adapts several practices used elsewhere:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <a
          href="https://www.win.ox.ac.uk/about/communications/wips/a-guide-to-preparing-for-your-wip/wip-guide/"
          >Oxford Centre for Integrative Neuroimaging</a
        > describes its WIPs as informal, early-stage opportunities for constructive support
        rather than tests or box-ticking exercises.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <a href="https://sites.uci.edu/biophysicsseminar/information-for-speakers/"
          >UC Irvine Biophysics and Systems Biology seminar</a
        > welcomes work ranging from an unpolished idea to prepublication and protects substantial
        time for discussion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.hopkinsmedicine.org/general-internal-medicine/useful-links/rip-tips"
          >Johns Hopkins General Internal Medicine</a
        > recommends stating one to three session objectives, focusing on a small number of
        dilemmas, using a note-taker, and treating feedback as advice rather than a mandate.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <a
          href="https://www.aera.net/Education-Research/Graduate-Student-Research-in-Progress-Roundtable-Series/RIPRS-Guidance-for-Presenters"
          >American Educational Research Association's research-in-progress roundtables</a
        > favor brief framing, a few selected aspects of the project, and questions designed
        to stimulate interaction.
      </li>
      <li>
        Golden and colleagues' <a
          href="https://journals.plos.org/ploscompbiol/article?id=10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008953"
          >“Ten simple rules for productive lab meetings”</a
        > emphasizes participant-defined objectives, clear roles, accessible meetings, and
        feedback that is open, supportive, respectful, and directed at ideas rather than people.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
